1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Let's talk Jev.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,000
So here's the thing, right?

3
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
A lot of us spent the last 2 years integrating Gen

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
AI into everything, into our search applications,

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,000
customer support chatbots or even programming logic.

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
But this last part, so dealing with logical or selection

7
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
problems with Gen AI models, is still a bit suboptimal.

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
For one, it can be hard to get Gen AI models to produce

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
an output that you can plug straight into a control flow.

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
You can ask the model to produce a structured output, like

11
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,000
a JSON or a TOML.

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
But at the end of the day, you're really just throwing that ball in the

13
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
air and praying to the AI gods that the results come back in the right shape.

14
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
And it's against this backdrop that TypeSafe AI launched Jev,

15
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
what they call a System One model.

16
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
At a high level, I think of Jev as a love child between

17
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
an old school classifier model and an LLM.

18
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Like an LLM, Jev can take any unstructured text

19
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
as input, but Jev's far more disciplined

20
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
about its outputs, meaning that it can only output a decision

21
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,000
format that you define ahead of time. So Jev

22
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
can output a Noul, which is a probability for a

23
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
yes-or-no question, a Choice, which is one option

24
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
from a closed list, or a Score against a rubric.

25
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,000
And this is great because it plugs directly into a mental model

26
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,000
of how programs and control flows actually work.

27
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Steve Faulkner from Cloudflare even proposed a programming

28
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
language wrapping Jev called Probably.

29
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Although, as this commenter pointed out, it was a missed opportunity to

30
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:33,000
call it JevaScript.

31
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Now, joking aside, the TypeSafe AI folks call

32
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Jev a System One model, like in Daniel Kahneman's

33
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
book, Thinking, Fast and Slow, because Jev is good at

34
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
making these decisions much faster than LLMs.

35
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
And just like Kahneman's System One thinking, Jev

36
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
isn't very good at things that require reasoning.

37
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
So in this video, let me show you some things that you can

38
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,000
do with Jev right now.

39
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Here's one that I think a lot of you will relate to, which is model routing.

40
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The core concept behind model routing is to use the right tool for the job.

41
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
So, if you have something simple, like a query rewrite, or

42
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,000
basic summarization, send it to a small model,

43
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
like an Anthropic Haiku, GPT Luna, or DeepSeek Flash.

44
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
But if you need something that requires complex reasoning, like planning

45
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
out a big software refactor, well, let's send that out to a larger

46
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,000
model, like a GPT Sol, Anthropic Opus, or Kimi-K3.

47
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
So you can imagine this is a really useful and valuable thing to do that

48
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,000
can potentially save you a lot of money.

49
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
And at the moment, a lot of people do this model routing

50
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
with a lightweight LLM like Claude Haiku.

